\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


For an entire function $f$, write
\[
 M(r,f)=\max_{|z|=r}|f(z)|,\qquad m_0(r,f)=\min_{|z|=r}|f(z)|.
\]
For nonconstant $f$, its order is $\rho(f)=\limsup_{r\to\infty}\log\log M(r,f)/\log r$;
the lower order is defined using $\liminf$ instead. Every constant entire function, including the zero function, has order and lower order zero. In logarithmic modulus ratios, use the extended-real convention $\log0=-\infty$.
For $\sigma\ge0$, let $\mathcal H_\sigma$ be the class of nonconstant entire functions such that
\[\limsup_{r\to\infty}\frac{\log M(r,f)}{(\log r)^2}\le\sigma.\]
Define the best universal lower bound
\[g_*(\sigma)=\inf_{f\in\mathcal H_\sigma}\limsup_{r\to\infty}\frac{m_0(r,f)}{M(r,f)}.\]

\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Determine $g_*(\sigma)$ for every $\sigma\ge0$. \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}{[S2]}
Update 2.52 records the sharp formula
\[
g_*(\sigma)=\left(\prod_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{1-q^{2n-1}}{1+q^{2n-1}}\right)^2,
\qquad q=e^{-1/(4\sigma)}\quad(\sigma>0),
\]
with the continuous value $g_*(0)=1$. It attributes the stronger meromorphic-function theorem to Gol'dberg and a different proof of the original entire-function problem to Fenton. \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Find the sharp minimum-to-maximum modulus ratio forced by a bound on the squared-logarithmic growth of an entire function.

